kovid may not have been in the headlines  for a while but it hasn't gone away far  from it as new data shows according to  the latest results from the office for  national statistics infection survey  more than three and a quarter million  people in the uk had coveted in the week  ending march the 12th that's about one  in every 21 people and after a steady  falling cases in the first two months of  the year the prevalence has been  increasing through march and the old  number is rising too so what's going on  well for a start our behavior has  relaxed the lifting of plan b at the end  of january led to a decline in face  masks lateral flow tests social  distancing and self-isolation and those  trends accelerated at the end of  february when the prime minister  outlined his plan to live with kovid and  that all gives the virus more  opportunities to spread but the virus  has changed too at the start of the year  the original omicron variant called ba  0.1 accounted for almost all covered  cases but in mid-february its closely  related spin-off called ba 0.2 here in  light blue began to take off and take  over now accounting for more than 75  percent of cases the two viruses are so  similar some scientists call them sister  variants but ba 0.2 is 1.4 times more  infectious and that's a rocket booster  that's driving up cases here in the uk  as well as hospital admissions but while  in earlier waves there was a lag between  people getting infected and then needing  emergency care this time the rise in  cases and hospitalizations happened at  the same time it suggests many patients  are going into hospital with the virus  not because of the virus it's still  disruptive for the nhs but public health  experts say the rise should be  short-lived  i do think that over the next  two to four weeks we will see a leveling  off as has been seen in other countries  so other countries have been ahead of us  nba too and we've seen a rapid downturn  in in infections in those countries  as as the population immunity has gained  covet is expected to become endemic  it'll come and go probably for decades  but for now immunity is good enough for  us to lead more normal lives
 

